@* Confirmation step for actions that can't be undone.

   This closes a real gap rather than adding ceremony: before this, DELETE on a rule and DELETE on a
   user both fired the API call on the first tap, with no confirmation and no undo - one mis-tap on a
   phone (where those buttons sat 60px wide inside a sideways-scrolling table) silently removed a rule
   from all 40 lab machines. Destructive + irreversible + no undo is exactly the case where a
   confirmation is worth the extra tap; nothing non-destructive in this app uses it.

   Design details that matter:
   - The dialog names the specific thing being deleted. "ลบกฎนี้?" is a question about an abstraction;
     "ลบกฎ Roblox?" is a question about a fact the user can check against what they meant to tap.
   - Cancel comes first and is the visually quieter option, so the muscle-memory "tap the right-hand
     button" reflex doesn't land on the destructive one.
   - The confirm button carries the verb ("ลบ"), not "OK" - a labelled action is understandable even
     if the reader skipped the sentence above it, which on a phone they usually did. *@
<div class="modal-backdrop" @onclick="OnCancel">
    <div class="modal modal-sm" @onclick:stopPropagation="true" role="alertdialog" aria-modal="true">
        <div class="modal-header">
            <span class="heading2">@Title</span>
        </div>

        <div style="margin-bottom:1.1rem">@Message</div>

        <div class="toolbar" style="margin-bottom:0">
            <span class="spacer"></span>
            <button class="btn btn-secondary" @onclick="OnCancel">ยกเลิก</button>
            <button class="btn @(Danger ? "btn-danger" : "btn-primary")" @onclick="OnConfirm">@ConfirmText</button>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Title { get; set; } = string.Empty;
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Message { get; set; } = string.Empty;
    [Parameter] public string ConfirmText { get; set; } = "ยืนยัน";

    /// <summary>true = paint the confirm button red. Reserved for genuinely destructive actions, so that
    /// red keeps meaning something instead of becoming the color every dialog happens to use.</summary>
    [Parameter] public bool Danger { get; set; } = true;

    [Parameter] public EventCallback OnConfirm { get; set; }
    [Parameter] public EventCallback OnCancel { get; set; }
}
